          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "if the number formed by one’s, ten’s and hundred’s digit is divisible by 8, then the number is divisible by 8."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.131,
            0.877,
            0.178
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Q. Change the last two digits of 8560 so that the resulting number is a multiple of 8.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Ans. 8552 is a multiple of 8."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.188,
            0.397,
            0.208
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "嶽 "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Q. Consider this statement:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.218,
            0.881,
            0.253
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Only the last three digits matter when deciding if a given number is divisible by 8.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.263,
            0.882,
            0.298
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. If the number formed by the last three digits is divisible by 8, then the original number is divisible by 8.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.308,
            0.882,
            0.343
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. If the original number is divisible by 8, then the number formed by the last three digits is divisible by 8.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.353,
            0.449,
            0.371
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Do you agree? Why or why not?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.381,
            0.238,
            0.452
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ans. a. Yes b. Yes c. Yes"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.462,
            0.661,
            0.479
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Yes. Some examples are: 8576,7648,5024. Try some more."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.515,
            0.269,
            0.537
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Figure it out"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.546,
            0.882,
            0.637
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Q.1. 2024 is a leap year (as February has 29 days). Leap years occurs in the years that are multiples of 4, except for those years that are evenly divisible by 100 but not 400. a. From the year you were born till now, which years were leap years? b. From the year 2024 till 2099, how many leap years are there?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.645,
            0.691,
            0.663
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ans. b. From the year 2024 till 2099, the number of leap years is 19"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.699,
            0.881,
            0.735
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Q.2. Find the largest and smallest 4-digit numbers that are divisible by 4 and are also palindromes.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.745,
            0.765,
            0.791
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ans. The largest 4-digit number divisible by 4 and also a palindrome: 9999 The smallest 4-digit number divisible by 4 and also a palindrome: 1001."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.826,
            0.881,
            0.917
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Q.3. Explore and find out if each statement is always true, sometimes true or never true. You can give examples to support your reasoning. a. Sum of two even numbers gives a multiple of 4. b. Sum of two odd numbers gives a multiple of 4.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
